      function r2more() {
        chain(show('r2more'), [
          ['By Torah law food and liquid don’t defile vessels. Why did the Sages decree that impure liquid does?', [
            { t: 'So people won’t be lax with a <b>zav’s or zava’s</b> fluids (spittle, urine), which are primary sources and do defile vessels.', ok: true, why: 'The decree guards the real Torah impurity.' },
            { t: 'Because liquids spread and reach every side.', ok: false, why: 'If that were the logic, the inside would be impure too.' },
            { t: 'To make mikveh vessels as strict as earthenware.', ok: false, why: 'Earthenware is the model, but it is not the reason.' }
          ], 'Bekhorot 38a:10'],
          ['And why did they leave the wooden cup’s inside pure, instead of making the whole cup impure as Torah law would?', [
            { t: 'As a <b>sign</b> that it is only rabbinic, so no one burns teruma or sacrificial food that touched it.', ok: true, why: 'By Torah law that food is pure and may not be burned. The half-measure tells everyone so.' },
            { t: 'Because impure liquid can’t reach the inside.', ok: false, why: 'It can. The ruling is a deliberate signal.' },
