          ], 'Bekhorot 38a:11-12'],
          ['Then why no sign for the flat bench? Ḥizkiyya makes both its sides impure.', [
            { t: 'The cup already made the point. Once people know liquid impurity is rabbinic, nobody will burn teruma over the bench.', ok: true, why: 'One sign is enough for the whole category.' },
            { t: 'A bench is never used for teruma.', ok: false, why: 'Not the Gemara’s answer.' },
            { t: 'Its impurity is Torah law.', ok: false, why: 'Liquid impurity is rabbinic here too.' }
          ], 'Bekhorot 38a:12-13'],
          ['A vessel must be “like a sack,” carried full and empty, to become impure at all. So which flat wooden vessels are susceptible?', [
            { t: 'Ones made for sitting, susceptible to <b>treading</b> (midras) by a zav, zava or menstruating woman.', ok: true, why: 'That is how a vessel without a receptacle comes to be susceptible.' },
            { t: 'Any wooden board.', ok: false, why: 'A flat board can’t be carried full, so it isn’t like a sack.' },
            { t: 'None. Ḥizkiyya meant earthenware after all.', ok: false, why: 'Earthenware without a receptacle never becomes impure.' }
